  "contributors": "Christopher Daniel Pang supplied the question about what a prime carries past its next collision and RPL and BLA accounting. Hearthline developed the expiring allocation model, adaptive group obstruction, quantitative bound and exact optimization under his ongoing construction key.",
  "model": "Blocks contain consecutive arriving primes. Ordinary cells belong only to the block whose distance interval released them. Unused ordinary cells expire at the block end and pocket draws purchase distinct positions. The final permissive block also sees the trailing columns through M. The causal control requires a cell by each arrival.",
  "paper_scope": "Even the minimum pocket draw after choosing every block length adaptively is bounded below by a constant times n*x*M/(r^4*log M), uniformly for all sufficiently large prescribed boards and 1 <= r <= exp(0.04*(log M)^(3/5)/(log log M)^(1/5)). Dividing by the allowed n^2/13 tends to infinity uniformly. The unrestricted shared-pool construction is still open.",
  "formal_scope": "The elementary separation of s*y from every whole column count simultaneously for 1<=s<=r, and the exact deduction from absolute discrepancies and their signed net to an unavoidable positive charge. The module imports stock Mathlib only. The asymptotic argument is written on paper.",
  "review": {
    "type": "author self-review",
    "sign": "A block draw is its positive demand minus allocation, clipped at zero. Twice the sum of draws equals total absolute imbalance plus the signed net. The proof bounds the actual global signed net separately.",
    "uniformity": "The log-price interval has width of order M/r^2. Its two endpoint errors and all errors for a short span of up to 4*r*log M are smaller than the specified separation. The restriction 4*0.04<0.18 pays the final comparison.",
    "partition": "Boundary-crossing groups and the extended terminal block are removed from the good-block lower bound. Even the within-group lookahead model has too large a charge. The causal replay spends actual released cells directly.",
    "notation": "Returned boards have x=4*sqrt(M)*(j+1)^2 and n=x^2. Both finite optima use the original n^2/13 allowance. No general memory bound for all algorithms or priority claim is inferred."
  },
